 Not Recommended (analyzed but impractical on 6502)

 Target: E2/E5 batch buffer newline scans
 Reason: Different addressing modes (indirect vs absolute), different output requirements, would clobber live BUF_PTR16
 ────────────────────────────────────────
 Target: H1-H4 counted movement loops
 Reason: 5-7 instructions each; parameterizing variable, boundary, and direction on 6502 costs more than the loop bodies
 Update (2026 audit): worth revisiting. Counted j and Ctrl-F need no loop at all: add the count to FILE_LINE16 and
   clamp to the last line (one clamp shared with G and dd). That form measured smaller than the loops and runs in O(1).
 ────────────────────────────────────────
 Target: N1/N2 string print
 Reason: write_fname caps the name at 32 chars (the status bar's text must fit STATUS_SHADOW); adding length cap to write_string would slow all callers
 ────────────────────────────────────────
 Target: F7/F9 indent/unindent
 Reason: Already share copy_line_to_nl; per-line logic (write spaces vs count/skip spaces) differs too much
 ────────────────────────────────────────
 Target: K1/K3 repeated multiplication
 Reason: 3-4 instruction bodies with different variable widths (16-bit vs 8-bit counter); parameterization exceeds loop
   size
 Update (2026 audit): worth revisiting. One shared shift-and-add 16x16 multiply replacing the repeated-addition loops
   measured about 90 bytes smaller, runs in O(bits) instead of O(count), and can report overflow, which the loops miss.
 ────────────────────────────────────────
 Target: G1/G2/G3 dispatch tables
 Reason: Different entry sizes (3 vs 5 bytes) and matching logic (single key vs two-key combo vs existence check)

 None of these would affect performance of expensive operations -- they're rejected purely because the consolidation
 overhead would exceed the code they replace.
